DATABASE STARTUP: DO NOT GUESS MISSING MIGRATION HISTORY
=======================================================

The startup migration script distinguishes a genuinely fresh application
database from an existing database with a known, single migration revision.
Missing metadata is not proof of an empty database. ORM create_all creates
missing tables but does not add missing columns to existing tables. Stamping
such a restored database to head can report success while required upgrades
never ran.

Supported automatic startup
---------------------------
- No migration table and no non-extension user relations: initialize the current
  schema, then stamp its revision. Pre-created empty schemas are allowed.
  PostgreSQL extension-owned relations are not treated as application tables.
- Exactly one known older revision: execute the actual incremental migrations.
- Exactly one current revision: no migration is needed. This is not a complete
  schema/data-integrity check or evidence that a customer restore is correct.

Startup refuses before changing schema when an unversioned database contains
ordinary, partitioned or foreign tables, views, materialized views or sequences
outside system schemas, unless they belong to an installed PostgreSQL extension.
This includes unrelated application objects: use a dedicated empty database for
a fresh installation. It also refuses empty, blank, multiple or unrecognized
revision metadata rather than choosing one value or treating it as a legacy
baseline. No relation names, row contents or credentials are included in these
refusal messages.

Fresh initialization is atomic
------------------------------
Creating the application schema and stamping its recognized revision now share
one PostgreSQL transaction. An exception or process termination before commit
rolls both operations back, including newly created application schemas. Empty
schemas that existed before startup remain intact. A normal retry can initialize
the database without deleting relations or inventing migration history.

If the transaction committed but its acknowledgment was lost, schema and revision
are committed together; the next startup recognizes that revision. This does not
make all existing-database migrations atomic: their established incremental path
and explicit autocommit requirements are unchanged. The advisory startup lock
still serializes cooperating starters.

This change does not automatically repair partial initialization left by an older
release or a genuinely incomplete restore. Existing unversioned relations still
refuse safely and require the recovery procedure below. Do not delete or stamp
them on the assumption that they came from an interrupted first installation.

Compatibility change
--------------------
Older startup code treated every unrecognized revision as pre-consolidation,
created missing model tables, re-stamped to 001_initial and replayed migrations.
That could also act on a future or divergent schema. This automatic guess is
removed. An installation with an unknown historical revision now requires its
matching source and a separately reviewed migration plan. Do not substitute an
arbitrary supported revision just to make startup proceed.

Known revisions in the shipped chain, including the published v26.09.0 revision,
remain eligible for normal incremental migration. A successful known-revision
upgrade does not cover custom schemas, missing metadata, arbitrary roles/ACLs,
database runtime conversion or a change of HA topology.

If startup refuses
------------------
1. Keep ingress and device/SMTP egress fenced. Stop all application writers,
   including API/worker/scheduler replicas, collectors, plugins and external
   maintenance clients. Retain the original volumes, images, configuration,
   migration logs and complete encrypted backups.
2. Identify the actual source image/release and how the database was restored.
   Missing or conflicting migration metadata can indicate an incomplete restore,
   mixed-version deployment or the wrong target database. Preserve those facts;
   do not delete existing tables, remove version rows or stamp to head.
3. Rehearse recovery into new isolated volumes from a verified complete backup.
   Establish that the recovered schema and migration history belong together.
   If the source revision is unknown to this release, obtain the corresponding
   source and review its actual schema transition. There is no force flag to
   replace this review.
4. Apply a compatible, reviewed migration with general workers stopped, then
   verify the resulting schema, representative data, constraints and application
   behavior before accepting writes. Keep original data until acceptance passes.

For database runtime conversion, use postgres/UPGRADING. For restored snapshots,
also follow TOKEN-REVOCATION-UPGRADING, redis/QUEUE-BACKUP-RECOVERY and
SLA-REPORT-DELIVERY. Preserve the vault encryption keys, coordinate fresh JWT
signing and mail recovery values, and reconcile non-JWT credentials and external
effects. Fixing migration metadata does not revoke old tokens or prevent replay.

Limits and evidence
-------------------
The advisory startup lock serializes cooperating FreeSDN migration processes.
It does not stop a database administrator or unrelated DDL writer, so the
maintenance fence is still required. The catalog check does not query a foreign
table's remote data. It is an accidental-recovery guard, not an access-control
boundary against a PostgreSQL administrator who can change catalogs or metadata.

Real PostgreSQL regression tests use an immutable old schema, verify that
ambiguous cases preserve original relations/columns and retained data, and
exercise fresh startup, extension membership, known incremental upgrades and
restart. These are synthetic checks; they are not hardware, HA, customer-data
completeness, independent-security-review or production RTO/RPO certification.
